<onlyinclude>The '''Johari window''' is a way to talk about what you know about yourself and what other people know about you. Its four areas are '''open''' (both know), '''blind''' (others know, you do not), '''hidden''' (you know, others do not) and '''unknown''' (neither knows). It is a conversation aid, not a personality diagnosis.</onlyinclude>

==Four areas==
{| class="wikitable"
! !! Known to yourself !! Not known to yourself
|-
! Known to others
| Open || Blind
|-
! Not known to others
| Hidden || Unknown
|}

For example, a colleague may know they are nervous about presenting but not tell anyone: this belongs in the hidden area for that audience. If teammates notice that the colleague interrupts people and the colleague has not noticed, that is a possible blind-area example. The categories describe knowledge in a particular relationship; they are not permanent labels.

==Using the model==
People may choose to share relevant information or request respectful feedback. A trusted conversation can change what is mutually known. However, feedback can be mistaken, privacy matters, and nobody should be pressured to disclose sensitive information.

==What it cannot tell you==
The diagram cannot measure the exact size of someone's personality, prove that a team has improved, or establish that feedback is accurate. It can help structure a discussion without settling these questions.

==Sources==
* Luft, J., and Ingham, H. (1955), ''The Johari Window: A Graphic Model of Interpersonal Awareness''.
